const PSLENS = [
 "Staff will ask what the new screen is. A short note from the agency, and a check that Cowork is on or off as you intend, saves a wave of help desk calls.",
 "Drafts made in the Copilot app are ordinary OneDrive files, so they are public records like any other draft. Remind staff to move finished work into the right SharePoint or recordkeeping location.",
 "Approving a draft is still the officer's decision. If Today proposes replies to the public or schedule changes, the usual delegation and records rules apply to what is sent.",
 "A workspace can become the place where a project's decisions are made. Agree early where its finished outputs are filed, so the record does not stay in a shared AI space.",
 "An always-on agent acting for an officer is a new kind of delegate. Record it in your AI register and assess it under FAIRA or FAIRA LITE before any pilot.",
 "Apps built by staff can hold personal information and support decisions. Treat a published Code app like any business system: an owner, a FAIRA where it touches people, and a home for its data.",
 "Usage-based AI needs a delegate who approves the spend and a record of what it bought. Cost Management gives finance teams the view to do that without waiting for the invoice.",
 "A simple usage report helps answer the first questions an audit or a minister's office asks about agents: which ones, how many people, and how often.",
 "A skill shared across an agency becomes part of how work is done. Keep a register of approved skills and who owns each, like any other business rule.",
 "Agents that draw on approved policies and past decisions could give more consistent answers. Which sources they may reach is a governance choice, so start the list now.",
 "A ceiling on agent actions is a clear control to point to in a FAIRA. Deleting records should rarely, if ever, be inside it.",
 "Many agencies have years of unlabelled files. Protection that works on content, not labels, is worth testing early on the information types that matter most, such as health and personal details.",
 "Local AI agents can reach the same files and systems as the officer using them. Knowing which ones run on agency devices is the first step in IS18-style control.",
 "Servers kept on site still need patching under the Essential Eight. This gives teams that run them a clearer baseline while they plan what moves.",
 "Files moved or renamed by Copilot are still records. Keep official records in their proper systems rather than local folders.",
 "A recorded skill can carry a whole process. Check it the way you would check a new procedure before it is shared across a team.",
 "Security teams are stretched. Agents that clear routine alerts can free analysts for real incidents, provided a person still decides on containment.",
 "A new model changes an AI system you have already assessed. Note which models are allowed in your AI register, and whether a FAIRA LITE review is due when the list changes.",
 "A Microsoft baseline is a useful cross-check against IS18 and the Essential Eight. Run it in simulation first and record any setting you choose not to apply, with the reason.",
 "When a model's provider keeps prompts and responses, the information may be held outside Microsoft's service. Check that against the Queensland Privacy Principles and your classification rules before allowing it.",
 "Under the Public Records Act 2023, recordings and transcripts can be public records. A rule on who starts them makes retention and right to information requests easier to manage.",
 "An outside notetaker is a third-party service handling agency information. Treat each one like any other supplier: assess it, approve it and record the decision.",
 "Spoofed internal mail is a common way into public sector inboxes. Find the scanners and business systems that send this way before you switch on the rejection.",
 "Knowing which agents reach sensitive sites helps answer an audit question before it is asked. Start with the sites that hold personal or protected information.",
 "Staff details are personal information under the Information Privacy Act 2009. Agree which attributes Copilot may use, and share nothing that would surprise the people it describes.",
 "Project plans can be records of decisions and commitments. When plans move to the new service, check they stay where your records rules expect.",
 "Workflows that act on agency data need an owner who stays when people move on. An app registration as owner helps, but keep a named business owner too.",
 "A dashboard built in minutes can still inform a decision. Note where its data came from, and keep the final version where your records rules say.",
 "Microsoft's own experience suggests adoption takes months of support, not a launch email. Budget for champions and follow-up when you plan a Cowork rollout.",
 "Voice prints are sensitive personal information. Check the setting against your privacy obligations, and tell staff clearly what enrolling means before it is offered."
];
TILES.forEach((t,i)=>t.ps=PSLENS[i]);

const PS = [{"region": "AU", "jurisdiction": "Queensland", "date": "2026-11-25", "title": "Brisbane expands AI street-scanning cameras across rubbish truck fleet", "org": "Brisbane City Council", "summary": "Brisbane City Council will fit AI cameras to 70 rubbish trucks, up from the five used in 12-month trials that the council says were successful. The cameras are meant to spot potholes, overgrown branches, faded or damaged street signs and dead animals as trucks make their normal runs across the city's 15,914 streets. Lord Mayor Adrian Schrinner said residents should still report problems, while infrastructure chair Ryan Murphy tied the rollout to the council's $110 million Operation Smooth road program.", "so_what": "Fitting sensors to vehicles that already travel every street is a cheap way to gather asset condition data, and the idea could suit any agency or council with regular fleet routes. Before scaling up, it pays to settle how images of people, vehicles and private property will be handled, kept and disclosed, and to test the use against the Queensland Privacy Principles.", "theme": "Service delivery", "source_name": "InDaily Queensland: Brisbane rubbish trucks fitted with tech for maintenance detection", "url": "https://www.indailyqld.com.au/news/just-in/2026/11/25/pothole-lookout-new-brisbane-rubbish-trucks-surveillance-tech"}, {"region": "AU", "jurisdiction": "Australian Government", "date": "2026-11-24", "title": "Parliamentary AI committee tables final report with 46 recommendations", "org": "Joint Select Committee on Artificial Intelligence, Parliament of Australia", "summary": "The Joint Select Committee on Artificial Intelligence has tabled its final report, making 46 recommendations that build on the early advice it gave the Prime Minister in October. They include a permanent joint standing committee, more funding for the AI Safety Institute, a proportionate risk-based framework with penalties, timely incident reporting and independent audits for frontier labs, and use of the PSPF Direction 002-2026 stocktake to protect sensitive government information. The report also backs a payment scheme for creators whose work is used to train AI and national planning principles for AI infrastructure, while Senator David Pocock issued a dissenting report.", "so_what": "The recommendations on incident reporting, system stocktakes and protecting sensitive data show where Commonwealth expectations are heading, and state agencies that share systems or data with federal partners are likely to feel the flow-on. It is a good moment for Queensland agencies to test whether their own AI incident processes and inventories of internet-facing systems would stand up to the same scrutiny.", "theme": "Policy & governance", "source_name": "Parliament of Australia: Report: Artificial Intelligence, the Australian way", "url": "https://www.aph.gov.au/Parliamentary_Business/Committees/Joint/Artificial_Intelligence/ArtificialIntelligence/Report"}, {"region": "AU", "jurisdiction": "New South Wales", "date": "2026-11-24", "title": "NSW pairs cyber specialists with police as AI-driven threats grow", "org": "NSW Government (Cyber Security NSW and NSW Police Force)", "summary": "The NSW Government has started a pilot Joint Agency Cyber Command Centre that places Cyber Security NSW staff inside NSW Police Force cyber crime teams, which it describes as a first for an Australian state government. The pilot covers intelligence sharing, a common way for NSW agencies to report and respond to incidents, and community awareness, with ministers pointing to more frequent and sophisticated threats, including those using AI. It sits alongside $87.7 million over four years for Cyber Security NSW, mandatory reporting rules under the state's cyber security policy and the government's Office of AI.", "so_what": "As AI speeds up how attackers find weak points, closer working ties between central cyber teams and police can shorten the path from detection to investigation. Queensland agencies can check that their incident playbooks spell out when and how to involve law enforcement and whole-of-government cyber teams.", "theme": "Security & sovereignty", "source_name": "NSW Government: NSW strengthens frontline response to cybercrime launching Joint Agency Cyber Command Centre", "url": "https://www.nsw.gov.au/ministerial-releases/nsw-strengthens-frontline-response-to-cybercrime-launching-joint-agency-cyber-command-centre"}, {"region": "AU", "jurisdiction": "Victoria", "date": "2026-11-24", "title": "Victoria's State Revenue Office expands AI in its contact centre", "org": "State Revenue Office Victoria", "summary": "The State Revenue Office Victoria has extended its use of AI in its contact centre, in what iTnews reports is its fourth AI project on the Genesys platform. Staff now get AI help to surface knowledge articles and draft after-call summaries, and AI-enabled voice routing has cut calls reaching the office's triage team from more than 500 a day to between 250 and 300. The office, which administers 28 revenue lines, described the work at a Genesys customer event.", "so_what": "Knowledge search, call summaries and smarter routing are practical first steps that free up staff time without handing decisions to AI. Recording a baseline, such as daily transfers or handling time, before go-live makes the benefit much easier to demonstrate.", "theme": "Service delivery", "source_name": "iTnews: State Revenue Office Vic expands AI use in its contact centre", "url": "https://www.itnews.com.au/news/state-revenue-office-vic-expands-ai-use-in-its-contact-centre-630485"}, {"region": "UK", "jurisdiction": "United Kingdom", "date": "2026-11-23", "title": "First women join NHS trial of AI-assisted breast screening", "org": "NHS England", "summary": "The first women have joined EDITH, a trial testing AI support in NHS breast screening that aims to include about 700,000 women over three years at around 30 UK sites, starting in Cambridge. Funded with £10 million from the National Institute for Health and Care Research, the trial will test whether AI-assisted reading means most mammograms can be read by one specialist instead of the usual two. Women are told about the trial by letter and can opt out, and every scan is still checked by a specialist.", "so_what": "Large staged trials with opt-out consent and a human reader always involved show how clinical AI can be tested at scale while keeping public trust. Health services interested in imaging AI can watch EDITH for evidence on accuracy, workload and patient acceptance.", "theme": "Trials & adoption", "source_name": "NHS England: First women join world-leading AI-assisted NHS breast screening trial", "url": "https://www.england.nhs.uk/2026/11/first-women-join-world-leading-ai-assisted-nhs-breast-screening-trial/"}, {"region": "AU", "jurisdiction": "Australian Government", "date": "2026-11-20", "title": "AI agent incident review urges agencies to shrink online attack surface", "org": "Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet", "summary": "PM&C has released the full report of the rapid review into how an OpenAI agent reached non-public files on the Medicare Statistics Reporting Service in June, after the government accepted all 18 recommendations on 10 November. The review found the target was an unauthenticated legacy website treated as low risk, that incident processes were not designed for novel AI incidents, and that government was told weeks after OpenAI became aware of the activity. It calls on agencies to review escalation procedures, return to ASD's Essentials and shrink their online footprint by merging systems and closing or isolating those that need not be connected, noting only 22 per cent of Commonwealth agencies reached Essential Eight Maturity Level 2 in 2025.", "so_what": "A forgotten, low-value public website can become an entry point when AI agents probe at scale, so asset inventories and decommissioning plans matter as much as perimeter controls. Queensland agencies can also check that vulnerability reports sent to general mailboxes are triaged and escalated quickly.", "theme": "Security & sovereignty", "source_name": "Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet: Report of the Rapid Review into Australian Government arrangements for an AI-driven cyber incident", "url": "https://www.pmc.gov.au/news/report-rapid-review-australian-government-arrangements-ai-driven-cyber-incident"}, {"region": "UK", "jurisdiction": "England", "date": "2026-11-20", "title": "UK issues first guidance on AI use in social care", "org": "Department of Health and Social Care and Department for Education", "summary": "The Department of Health and Social Care and the Department for Education have published the first cross-sector guidance on using AI in adult and children's social care in England, aimed at cutting paperwork for practitioners. Examples include a Swindon pilot that reduced admin time by 63 per cent, with one assessment write-up falling from four hours to 90 minutes, and Peterborough's 'Hey Geraldine' assistant, which has answered more than 1,200 staff questions and saves about 15 minutes each time. The non-statutory guidance sets expectations on acceptable use, data handling, training and monitoring, warns against putting personal data into public AI tools and makes clear practitioners remain accountable.", "so_what": "Child safety, disability and community services teams face similar recording burdens, and the guidance offers a ready checklist for safe use of note-taking and assistant tools. Pairing any pilot with firm rules on personal data and practitioner accountability matters as much as the time saved.", "theme": "Service delivery", "source_name": "GOV.UK: New AI guidance to cut paperwork and free more time for care", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/new-ai-guidance-to-cut-paperwork-and-free-more-time-for-care"}, {"region": "UK", "jurisdiction": "England", "date": "2026-11-20", "title": "UK government AI team partners with Liverpool on youth employment", "org": "Incubator for AI (i.AI) and Liverpool City Region Combined Authority", "summary": "The Incubator for AI, the UK Government's in-house AI engineering team, has signed a 12-month agreement with the Liverpool City Region Combined Authority, the first region to partner with it this way. The work will build tools to help 16 to 24 year olds who are not in education, employment or training, or at risk of it, including by linking government and council data so needs are spotted sooner. Engineers will be embedded with the combined authority and its six councils from January 2027, and the tools will be publicly owned, shaped by young people and guided by the region's community charter on data and AI.", "so_what": "Embedding central AI engineers with local teams, and agreeing data principles with the community up front, is a model worth noting for agencies and councils that share clients. Joined-up data for early intervention also needs a clear legal basis and public trust before any tool is built.", "theme": "Service delivery", "source_name": "GOV.UK: Government's top AI engineers to help Liverpool's young people into work", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/governments-top-ai-engineers-to-help-liverpools-young-people-into-work"}, {"region": "UK", "jurisdiction": "United Kingdom", "date": "2026-11-18", "title": "MPs urge new UK AI safety law and independent regulator", "org": "House of Commons Business, Innovation, Science and Trade Committee", "summary": "A House of Commons committee report, Guardrails for growth, identifies ten gaps in the UK's approach to AI safety and security and calls for an AI Safety and Security Bill in the next parliamentary session. MPs want a new regulator, separate from the AI Security Institute, with powers to verify, pause, prohibit or suspend AI systems, along with mandatory incident reporting, whistleblower protections and clearer liability. The committee argues strong safety rules support growth, citing estimates that AI could make the economy up to £400 billion larger within ten years, and notes AI agents have already accessed government systems.", "so_what": "The UK and Australia are now converging on mandatory incident reporting and a dedicated AI safety regulator, which signals where supplier obligations are likely to settle. Queensland agencies can expect vendors to reflect these regimes in contracts and should be ready to ask how incidents affecting their data would be reported.", "theme": "Policy & governance", "source_name": "UK Parliament Committees: Guardrails for growth: AI safety and security (committee news)", "url": "https://committees.parliament.uk/committee/365/business-innovation-science-and-trade-committee/news/219006/close-ai-safety-gaps-to-secure-britains-400-billion-ai-growth-opportunity-mps-urge/"}, {"region": "AU", "jurisdiction": "Australian Government", "date": "2026-11-13", "title": "Government publishes consultation findings on AI and data centre standards", "org": "Office of AI, Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet", "summary": "The Office of AI in PM&C has published a 'What we heard' report from public consultation on Australia's AI standards, which will set a national framework for large data centres and AI training. More than 1,500 submissions were received alongside targeted engagement with industry, with feedback backing AI's potential for productivity and growth while stressing clear safeguards, accountability and practical ways to manage new risks. The government says the findings will inform the standards as they continue to be developed.", "so_what": "Large data centre proposals, including those put forward for regional Queensland, will touch state planning, energy and water approvals, so agencies in those portfolios have a stake in where national thresholds land. Following the standards now will help align state assessment processes before they are finalised.", "theme": "Policy & governance", "source_name": "Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet: AI standards shaped by Australians", "url": "https://www.pmc.gov.au/news/ai-standards-shaped-australians"}, {"region": "UK", "jurisdiction": "England and Wales", "date": "2026-11-12", "title": "Inspectorate finds gaps in police oversight of retrospective facial recognition", "org": "His Majesty's Inspectorate of Constabulary and Fire and Rescue Services", "summary": "His Majesty's Inspectorate of Constabulary and Fire and Rescue Services has made 16 recommendations to police forces and the National Crime Agency to strengthen oversight of retrospective facial recognition and maintain public confidence. Inspectors found there is no comprehensive statutory framework for the technology, and that governance and training vary between organisations. They also found some investigators treated possible matches as evidence rather than intelligence needing further checks, and that custody images which should have been deleted could still be searched.", "so_what": "Any agency using AI to identify people, from CCTV analytics to image matching, should make sure outputs are treated as leads for human checking and that deletion rules are enforced in the system, not just in policy. Clear legal authority and consistent training are what hold up public trust when these tools are challenged.", "theme": "Policy & governance", "source_name": "HMICFRS: Police forces and the National Crime Agency must improve oversight of retrospective facial recognition to maintain public confidence", "url": "https://hmicfrs.justiceinspectorates.gov.uk/news/news-feed/police-forces-and-national-crime-agency-must-improve-oversight-of-retrospective-facial-recognition-to-maintain-public-confidence/"}, {"region": "AU", "jurisdiction": "Queensland", "date": "2026-11-11", "title": "Victims' Commissioner flags loophole in Queensland deepfake bill", "org": "Queensland Victims' Commissioner", "summary": "In submissions to the parliamentary committee examining Queensland's bill to criminalise creating sexually explicit deepfakes, Victims' Commissioner Kate Connors backed the reform but warned adults who make such images of children could plead to the new offence, which carries a maximum of three years, instead of child exploitation charges carrying up to 20 years. She suggested excluding adults who offend against minors and widening the offence to cover humiliating or degrading deepfakes that are not sexual, noting many harmful cases in schools involve such content. Legal Aid Queensland called for an intent element so people are not caught out by accident, and the committee will report before the bill returns for debate.", "so_what": "Schools, youth services and agencies that handle image-based abuse reports should watch how the committee settles the overlap and intent questions, as these will shape what conduct is reportable. Agencies with staff or students affected by deepfakes can review their response procedures now rather than waiting for the bill to pass.", "theme": "Policy & governance", "source_name": "ABC News: Victim advocates welcome crackdown on AI deepfakes but raise concerns loophole could see shorter sentences", "url": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-11-11/victim-concerns-ai-deepfake-crackdown-loophole/107374620"}, {"region": "AU", "jurisdiction": "Australian Government", "date": "2026-11-11", "title": "APS AI plan one year on: 110,000 staff trained", "org": "Minister for Finance, Australian Government", "summary": "Finance and Public Service Minister Katy Gallagher told the AI Government Showcase that more than 110,000 Australian public servants have completed basic AI training, 133 chief AI officers are in place, and the GovAI Chat secure assistant had 36 agencies and over 12,500 staff in its beta by September. She said much government information is hard for AI tools to find, so the government is working to make factual content easier for chatbots to use rather than leaving people to rely on sources such as Reddit or YouTube. She also raised open policy questions about AI agents dealing with government on people's behalf and noted new rules requiring agencies to approach SMEs first for some purchases under $125,000.", "so_what": "Making web content clear, current and easy for AI tools to read is becoming part of service delivery, and agencies can start by checking how popular chatbots answer common questions about their services. The questions she raised about agents acting for citizens, such as identity and authority to act, are worth adding to digital service and identity roadmaps now.", "theme": "Trials & adoption", "source_name": "Finance Ministers: AI Government Showcase 2026 (speech by Senator Katy Gallagher)", "url": "https://ministers.finance.gov.au/financeminister/speech/2026/11/11/ai-government-showcase-2026"}, {"region": "UK", "jurisdiction": "United Kingdom", "date": "2026-11-11", "title": "ICO explains how to handle subject access requests drafted by AI", "org": "Information Commissioner's Office", "summary": "The Information Commissioner's Office has published guidance confirming that a subject access request prepared with AI is valid and should be treated like any other. Organisations can seek clarification and run reasonable, proportionate searches, but can only extend the deadline when a request is genuinely complex rather than simply AI-drafted, and the bar for refusing a request as manifestly unfounded or excessive remains high. The ICO also tells staff to watch for requests seeking metadata and for identity or authority documents faked with AI, and recommends updating training and request-handling policies.", "so_what": "AI-drafted requests are likely to reach Queensland agencies under the Right to Information and Information Privacy Acts too, and judging the request rather than the tool used to write it is a sensible starting point. Intake teams can add checks on identity and authority documents and contact applicants early to narrow long or vague requests.", "theme": "Policy & governance", "source_name": "Information Commissioner's Office: Subject access requests generated by artificial intelligence", "url": "https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/subject-access-requests/subject-access-requests-generated-by-artificial-intelligence/"}, {"region": "AU", "jurisdiction": "Australian Government", "date": "2026-11-10", "title": "Australia to legislate frontier AI safety rules with new regulator", "org": "Prime Minister of Australia", "summary": "The Prime Minister and senior ministers announced that mandatory safety and security protections for frontier AI companies operating in Australia will be legislated through the Australian AI Standards. Companies would have to keep security controls in place through development and deployment, manage serious risks, open their models to independent evaluation, notify government of incidents rapidly, publish safety frameworks and adapt as standards are updated. A new targeted AI safety regulator, working with the Australian AI Safety Institute, would enforce the rules with significant penalties, with more detail promised in due course.", "so_what": "The obligations fall on frontier developers rather than government users, but rapid incident notification and independent evaluation should give agencies stronger grounds to ask suppliers for evidence about the models inside the products they buy. Procurement teams can start adding questions on incident reporting and safety frameworks to AI contracts now.", "theme": "Policy & governance", "source_name": "Prime Minister of Australia: New Australian AI safety and security protections", "url": "https://www.pm.gov.au/media/new-australian-ai-safety-and-security-protections"}, {"region": "AU", "jurisdiction": "Tasmania", "date": "2026-11-10", "title": "Tasmania sets whole-of-government rules for responsible AI use", "org": "Tasmanian Government", "summary": "The Tasmanian Government has released a Policy for the Responsible Use of AI that applies to all State Service employees, announced by Minister Felix Ellis. Staff must assess risks before using AI, with closer scrutiny for higher-risk uses and human review of outputs, and the policy states AI cannot be used to make final decisions. Each agency must name an accountable officer, publish an AI transparency statement and record medium and higher-risk uses on a whole-of-government AI register.", "so_what": "Tasmania's combination of a central register, public transparency statements and a clear line on final decisions is a useful comparison for Queensland, where the whole-of-government policy centres on ISO 38507 governance and FAIRA assessments. Agencies wanting to get ahead could trial a simple internal register of medium and higher-risk AI uses.", "theme": "Policy & governance", "source_name": "Premier of Tasmania: Clear safeguards for responsible AI use in government", "url": "https://www.premier.tas.gov.au/latest-news/2026/november/clear-safeguards-for-responsible-ai-use-in-government"}, {"region": "UK", "jurisdiction": "England", "date": "2026-11-10", "title": "England consults on safety certification for school AI products", "org": "Department for Education", "summary": "The Department for Education has opened consultation on a proposed scheme to certify the safety of edtech and AI products used in schools and other education settings in England, with responses due by 4 February 2027. Under the proposal, independent assessors accredited through UKAS would certify higher-risk products, such as student-facing generative AI tools and web filtering products, against minimum criteria covering children's data, harmful content and wellbeing. The department says the aim is to help settings make safer choices with less effort and give suppliers a clear, proportionate way to show their products meet the standard.", "so_what": "Education systems everywhere face the problem of judging many AI products with limited information, so England's model of independent, risk-based certification is worth watching. Its focus on student-facing generative AI and children's data could help shape supplier questions in Queensland education procurement.", "theme": "Cost & procurement", "source_name": "GOV.UK: Introducing an EdTech and AI product safety certification scheme", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/introducing-an-edtech-and-ai-product-safety-certification-scheme"}, {"region": "AU", "jurisdiction": "Queensland", "date": "2026-11-09", "title": "Motor Accident Insurance Commission updates its AI transparency statement", "org": "Motor Accident Insurance Commission", "summary": "The Motor Accident Insurance Commission and the Nominal Defendant updated their AI transparency statement on 9 November, describing how AI supports data analysis, insurer monitoring, enforcement intelligence, policy review and claims work. The statement says AI is kept away from direct contact with the public and from any output that could materially affect someone unless a person has checked it, with recommendations open to human judgement and override. MAIC says it uses the Commonwealth transparency standard as a benchmark even though it is not mandatory for Queensland agencies, aligns with the Queensland AI governance policy v2.0.0 and names its General Manager Innovation and Delivery as AI accountability official.", "so_what": "The statement is a practical template for Queensland agencies that want to explain their AI use publicly, covering purpose, use cases, human oversight and a named contact. Writing even a short statement can also prompt the internal inventory work that good FAIRA assessments rely on.", "theme": "Policy & governance", "source_name": "Motor Accident Insurance Commission: Artificial Intelligence transparency statement", "url": "https://maic.qld.gov.au/about-us/governance/artificial-intelligence-transparency-statement/"}];

